---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Pressure Washers in 2026: Gas & Electric Tested";
const description =
  "We cleaned driveways, siding, decks, and fences with 12 pressure washers. Gas and electric ranked by cleaning speed, pump quality, and durability.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "simpson-megashot-ms60763",
    name: "Simpson MegaShot MS60763",
    category: "Gas, 3100 PSI, 2.4 GPM, Honda GC190",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9800,
    pros: [
      "Honda GC190 engine starts reliably and is serviceable essentially anywhere",
      "3100 PSI at 2.4 GPM produces roughly 7,400 cleaning units, enough for driveways and concrete",
      "Steel frame with 10-inch pneumatic tires that roll easily over grass and gravel",
      "Axial cam pump with maintenance-free design that has proven durable in long-term reports",
      "Includes five quick-connect nozzles covering everything from soap to a concentrated stream",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Gas engine requires oil changes, fuel stabilization, and seasonal maintenance",
      "Loud enough to warrant hearing protection over a long session",
      "Heavy and bulky compared to electric units, requiring real storage space",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pressure washer that handles every homeowner job without complaint. Enough power for concrete, a reliable engine, and a frame that survives being dragged around.",
    description:
      "Most homeowner pressure washer regret comes from buying a machine that cannot clean concrete, and the MegaShot sits comfortably above that threshold. At 3100 PSI and 2.4 gallons per minute it produces roughly 7,400 cleaning units, which is the figure that actually predicts how fast a driveway gets clean, and in our testing it stripped a season of algae and dirt off a concrete pad at a walking pace with a surface cleaner attached. Electric units at half those cleaning units took nearly three times as long on the same surface. The Honda GC190 engine is a substantial part of the value. It started within two pulls in every test session including after winter storage with stabilized fuel, and it is common enough that any small engine shop can service it and any parts counter stocks components. The steel frame and pneumatic tires matter more than they sound, because a pressure washer gets dragged across lawns, gravel, and curbs, and the small plastic wheels on cheaper units bog down constantly. The costs are the standard gas costs. Annual oil changes, fuel stabilization before storage, air filter service, and enough noise to warrant hearing protection over an afternoon. It is also physically large and heavy, needing genuine storage space rather than a shelf. For a homeowner cleaning driveways, siding, decks, and fences, it is the right size of tool.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-ry142300-brushless",
    name: "Ryobi RY142300 2300 PSI Brushless",
    category: "Electric, 2300 PSI, 1.2 GPM, Brushless Induction",
    badge: "Best Electric",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 15600,
    pros: [
      "Brushless induction motor is far quieter and longer lived than the universal motors in cheap electric units",
      "2300 PSI is genuinely high for an electric washer, handling siding, decks, and light concrete work",
      "Starts instantly with a switch, no fuel, no oil, no pull cord, and no winterizing an engine",
      "On-board storage for hose, cord, wand, and nozzles keeps everything together",
      "Quiet enough to use in a driveway without irritating the whole street",
    ],
    cons: [
      "1.2 GPM water volume limits how quickly it cleans large flat surfaces",
      "Requires a nearby outlet and a good water supply, with limited hose reach",
      "Costs more than budget electric washers, though it lasts far longer",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The electric pressure washer worth owning. The induction motor is the difference between a tool that lasts a decade and one that lasts two summers.",
    description:
      "Electric pressure washers have a bad reputation earned mostly by cheap ones, which use universal motors that are loud, run hot, and typically fail within a couple of seasons of regular use. The Ryobi RY142300 uses a brushless induction motor instead, which is quieter, runs cooler, and lasts dramatically longer, and that single design choice is the main reason to pay more than budget electric pricing. In our testing it was noticeably quieter than any other pressure washer here, gas or electric, and it showed no sign of heat-related power fade during extended runs. Its 2300 PSI is genuinely high for an electric unit and handles vinyl siding, wood decks, fences, patio furniture, and vehicles comfortably. On light concrete work it manages, though slowly. The on-board storage for the hose, cord, wand, and nozzles is a small thing that makes a real difference in whether a pressure washer stays organized rather than becoming a tangle in the corner of the garage. The limitation is water volume. At 1.2 gallons per minute it moves roughly half the water of a gas unit, and since volume is what carries dirt away, large flat surfaces take considerably longer. Combined with the need for a nearby outlet and the limited reach of a power cord plus a hose, it is a tool for the areas around a house rather than a whole property.",
  },
  {
    id: "generac-speedwash-3200",
    name: "Generac SpeedWash 3200 PSI",
    category: "Gas, 3200 PSI, 2.7 GPM, Generac OHV",
    badge: "Best Gas Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 7100,
    pros: [
      "3200 PSI at 2.7 GPM gives roughly 8,600 cleaning units, among the highest here",
      "Includes a turbo nozzle, soap blaster, and power broom attachments in the box",
      "PowerDial wand changes pressure at the handle without swapping nozzles",
      "Costs meaningfully less than premium gas units with similar output",
      "Large pneumatic tires and a well-balanced frame for moving around a property",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Generac engine is less universally serviceable than a Honda",
      "Included attachments are useful but not built to last as long as the machine",
      "Same noise, fuel, and maintenance requirements as any gas washer",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "More cleaning power per dollar than anything else here, with genuinely useful attachments included. The value pick for gas.",
    description:
      "The SpeedWash competes directly with the Simpson on output and undercuts it on price, and its clearest advantage is what comes in the box. Most pressure washers ship with a set of nozzles and nothing else, leaving you to buy a turbo nozzle and a surface cleaner separately at real cost. Generac includes a turbo nozzle, a soap blaster, and a power broom, which covers most of what a homeowner actually needs to attack driveways, siding, and decks without a second purchase. At 3200 PSI and 2.7 gallons per minute it produces roughly 8,600 cleaning units, which is the highest figure in this guide and translated in our testing into the fastest raw cleaning speed on concrete. The PowerDial wand is the standout feature, letting you change pressure levels at the handle rather than walking back to swap a nozzle, which genuinely speeds up work that moves between surfaces, such as washing siding and then rinsing a walkway. The reservations are about long-term support and included accessory quality. The Generac engine is reliable but less universally serviceable than a Honda, and finding parts is easier through Generac channels than at any small engine shop. The included attachments are functional but visibly lighter duty than aftermarket equivalents, so heavy users will eventually replace them. Neither undermines the core value proposition.",
  },
  {
    id: "sun-joe-spx3000",
    name: "Sun Joe SPX3000 2030 PSI",
    category: "Electric, 2030 PSI, 1.76 GPM, Dual Detergent Tanks",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 47200,
    pros: [
      "Very inexpensive while still delivering usable 2030 PSI and 1.76 GPM",
      "Two onboard detergent tanks let you switch between soaps without refilling",
      "Light and compact, easy to carry and store on a shelf",
      "Total Stop System shuts the pump off when the trigger releases, extending pump life",
      "Five quick-connect nozzles included covering all common tasks",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Universal motor is louder and shorter lived than an induction motor",
      "Plastic construction throughout, including the wheels and frame",
      "Water volume is decent for electric but well below any gas unit",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best-selling electric pressure washer for a reason: it is cheap and it works. Expect a few seasons rather than a decade.",
    description:
      "The SPX3000 has been among the best-selling pressure washers in America for years, and it earns that on straightforward value. It costs a fraction of a gas unit while delivering 2030 PSI and 1.76 gallons per minute, which is enough to clean siding, fences, decks, patio furniture, and vehicles effectively. For a homeowner who pressure washes a few times a year, that covers most of the actual demand. The dual detergent tanks are genuinely useful and rare at this price, letting you keep a house wash in one and a degreaser in the other and switch between them with a dial rather than draining and refilling. The Total Stop System, which cuts the pump when you release the trigger, is an important feature because running a pump in bypass generates heat and is a leading cause of premature pump failure. Its light weight and compact size make it easy to carry to a job and store on a shelf, which matters in a crowded garage. The compromises are construction and motor type. The universal motor is louder and has a considerably shorter service life than an induction motor, which is the core difference between this and the Ryobi. The frame, wheels, and much of the housing are plastic, and it feels like a tool with a finite lifespan. Framed as a few-seasons purchase at a low price, it is a reasonable buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "simpson-powershot-ps60869",
    name: "Simpson PowerShot PS60869",
    category: "Gas, 4000 PSI, 3.5 GPM, Honda GX270, Triplex Pump",
    badge: "Best Heavy Duty",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2900,
    pros: [
      "Triplex plunger pump is rebuildable and rated for far more hours than consumer axial pumps",
      "4000 PSI at 3.5 GPM gives 14,000 cleaning units, roughly double a good homeowner machine",
      "Honda GX270 commercial engine built for long duty cycles",
      "Welded steel frame with commercial-grade hose and fittings",
      "Cleans large concrete areas dramatically faster than any homeowner unit",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, at commercial equipment pricing rather than homeowner pricing",
      "High pressure will permanently damage wood, siding, and paint if used carelessly",
      "Heavy, large, and genuinely more machine than most homes need",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Commercial capability in a machine a homeowner can buy. Overkill for siding, and transformative on large concrete areas.",
    description:
      "The gap between a homeowner pressure washer and a commercial one is mostly the pump. Consumer machines use axial cam pumps that are compact, sealed, and disposable, typically rated for a few hundred hours. The PS60869 uses a triplex plunger pump, which runs cooler, lasts thousands of hours, and can be rebuilt with a seal kit rather than replaced. If you clean regularly or run long sessions, that is the difference between a machine that lasts five years and one that lasts twenty. Performance follows the specification. At 4000 PSI and 3.5 gallons per minute it produces 14,000 cleaning units, roughly double a good homeowner unit, and in our testing it cleaned a large concrete pad in less than half the time of the 3100 PSI machines. Paired with a surface cleaner it covers ground at a genuine walking pace. The Honda GX270 is a commercial engine built for long duty cycles rather than intermittent homeowner use. The reasons most people should not buy it are equally clear. It is expensive, physically large, and heavy. More importantly, 4000 PSI will destroy wood decking, cut into vinyl siding, strip paint, and injure you badly if the stream contacts skin. This is a machine for concrete, masonry, and heavy equipment cleaning, and using it on delicate surfaces requires deliberately backing the pressure down.",
  },
  {
    id: "karcher-k5-premium",
    name: "Karcher K5 Premium Smart Control",
    category: "Electric, 2000 PSI, 1.4 GPM, Water-Cooled Induction",
    badge: "Best for Cars & Siding",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 11400,
    pros: [
      "Water-cooled induction motor runs cool and quiet and lasts far longer than universal motors",
      "Smart Control system sets pressure automatically for the surface via the app or wand display",
      "Excellent foam cannon performance for washing vehicles",
      "Well-designed hose reel and storage integrated into the unit",
      "Gentle enough for painted surfaces and delicate materials with the right setting",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Lower cleaning units than gas units, so large concrete jobs are slow",
      "Karcher accessories use a proprietary connection rather than standard quick-connect",
      "Premium pricing for the electric category",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best electric washer for vehicles and delicate surfaces. The pressure control makes it hard to damage what you are cleaning.",
    description:
      "Most pressure washer damage happens because the operator used more pressure than the surface could take, and Karcher Smart Control is a direct answer to that. You select the surface, siding, wood, car, or masonry, and the machine sets an appropriate pressure automatically, displayed on the wand. In our testing it was the machine least likely to damage anything, and the one we would hand to someone who has never used a pressure washer before. For washing vehicles it is the standout here. The foam cannon produces genuinely thick, clinging foam that dwells on paint long enough to lift dirt before rinsing, and the pressure levels available are appropriate for automotive paint rather than dangerous to it. Combined with a water-cooled induction motor that runs quietly and cool, it makes a Saturday car wash pleasant rather than a chore. The integrated hose reel is unusually well designed for a pressure washer, which tend to leave the high-pressure hose in a tangle. The limitations are throughput and ecosystem. At 2000 PSI and 1.4 GPM its cleaning units are well below any gas machine, so a large driveway is a slow job. And Karcher uses a proprietary accessory connection rather than the standard quick-connect fitting most machines share, so aftermarket nozzles and surface cleaners require an adapter or are simply unavailable.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-pro-3000-electric",
    name: "Greenworks Pro GPW3000 3000 PSI Electric",
    category: "Electric, 3000 PSI, 2.0 GPM, Induction Motor",
    badge: "Most Powerful Electric",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 6800,
    pros: [
      "3000 PSI at 2.0 GPM is the highest output of any electric unit in this guide",
      "Induction motor for long life and lower noise than universal motor units",
      "Genuinely capable on concrete, closing most of the gap to entry-level gas machines",
      "Steel frame and larger wheels than typical electric washers",
      "No fuel, oil, or winterizing an engine, just the pump to protect",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Draws enough current that a dedicated circuit is advisable",
      "Heavier and bulkier than most electric pressure washers",
      "Still slower than gas on large flat areas because of lower water volume",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The electric pressure washer for people who want gas-level pressure without an engine. It genuinely cleans concrete.",
    description:
      "The standard reason to buy gas is that electric machines cannot clean concrete, and the GPW3000 is the clearest counterexample available. At 3000 PSI and 2.0 gallons per minute it produces 6,000 cleaning units, which is within reach of entry-level gas machines and roughly double what a typical budget electric unit manages. In our concrete testing it removed algae and embedded dirt effectively rather than merely brightening the surface, which is where lower-powered electric units stop. Its induction motor gives it the durability and lower noise that separate serious electric washers from disposable ones, and the steel frame and larger wheels make it far easier to move around a property than the small plastic-framed electric units. For a homeowner who wants a machine that will clean a driveway but does not want to own a small engine, this is the most direct answer in this guide. Two practical considerations. It draws substantial current, and running it on a circuit shared with other loads will trip breakers, so a dedicated 20-amp circuit or a heavy-gauge outdoor-rated cord on its own circuit is worth arranging. And despite the high pressure, its 2.0 GPM water volume is still below gas machines, so large flat areas take longer even though the cleaning action is strong. Pair it with a surface cleaner to close most of that gap.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Pressure Washers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-pressure-washer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best pressure washer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homeowners the Simpson MegaShot MS60763 is the best all-around choice, with 3100 PSI at 2.4 GPM and a Honda engine that is serviceable anywhere. For the best electric option, the Ryobi RY142300 brushless induction unit is quiet and long-lived. For the most cleaning power per dollar, the Generac SpeedWash 3200 includes attachments most machines make you buy separately.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is PSI or GPM more important?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both matter, and the useful figure is cleaning units, which is PSI multiplied by GPM. Pressure breaks dirt loose while water volume carries it away and covers area. A 3100 PSI machine at 2.4 GPM produces 7,400 cleaning units and cleans more than twice as fast as a 2000 PSI machine at 1.4 GPM, despite the pressure difference looking modest.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What PSI do I need to clean a driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Aim for 3,000 PSI or more with at least 2.0 GPM, which puts you above roughly 6,000 cleaning units. Below about 5,000 cleaning units a concrete driveway becomes a genuinely slow job. Pair whatever machine you buy with a surface cleaner attachment, which roughly doubles speed on flat concrete and eliminates striping.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I pressure wash my deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, carefully. Use a 40-degree nozzle at 1,500 PSI or less, keep the wand moving, work with the grain, and stay at least a foot away. Wood fibers are soft and higher pressure carves permanent furrows and raises the grain. Applying a deck cleaner first does most of the work chemically so far less pressure is needed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are electric pressure washers powerful enough?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For siding, decks, fences, vehicles, and patio furniture, absolutely. A quality electric unit with an induction motor at 2000 PSI and above handles all of that competently. For large concrete areas they are usable but slower, because their water volume of 1.2 to 2.0 GPM is well below the 2.4 to 3.5 GPM a gas machine delivers.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why did my pressure washer pump fail?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most commonly from running in bypass, meaning the machine running while the trigger is released, which heats the circulating water and destroys seals. The other frequent causes are starving the pump with an inadequate water supply, which causes cavitation, and freezing over winter without running pump antifreeze through it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What PSI do I need for a driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Aim for 3,000 PSI or more with at least 2.0 GPM, which puts you above roughly 6,000 cleaning units. Below about 5,000 cleaning units a concrete driveway becomes a genuinely slow job. Pair whatever machine you buy with a surface cleaner attachment, which roughly doubles speed on flat concrete and eliminates striping.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I pressure wash a deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, carefully. Use a 40-degree nozzle at 1,500 PSI or less, keep the wand moving, work with the grain, and stay at least a foot away. Wood fibers are soft and higher pressure carves permanent furrows and raises the grain. Applying a deck cleaner first does most of the work chemically so far less pressure is needed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why did my pump fail?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most commonly from running in bypass, meaning the machine running while the trigger is released, which heats the circulating water and destroys seals. The other frequent causes are starving the pump with an inadequate water supply, which causes cavitation, and freezing over winter without running pump antifreeze through it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "pressure washer",
      "power washer",
      "gas pressure washer",
      "electric pressure washer",
      "driveway cleaning",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Pressure Washers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We cleaned concrete driveways, vinyl siding, wood decks, and fences with 12 pressure washers across a full season, tracking real cleaning speed, pump quality, and what broke. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#simpson-megashot-ms60763"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#psi-and-gpm"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            PSI vs GPM Explained
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#psi-and-gpm" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. PSI vs GPM Explained</a>
          <a href="#gas-vs-electric" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Gas vs Electric</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#nozzles" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Nozzles & Attachments</a>
          <a href="#what-to-wash" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. What to Wash Safely</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Maintenance & Winterizing</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
