      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pressure washers are sold almost entirely on PSI, which is the number least likely to determine how fast you finish a driveway. Water volume does more of the work than pressure does, and the wrong nozzle at the wrong pressure will carve permanent stripes into concrete and strip paint off siding. Here is what the specifications actually mean and which machines earn their price.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: PSI, GPM, and Cleaning Units -->
        <div id="psi-and-gpm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. PSI, GPM, and Cleaning Units
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure washers are marketed on PSI almost exclusively, which is misleading, because pressure alone does not determine how fast a surface gets clean. Two numbers matter and they work together.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>PSI is pounds per square inch, the force of the water stream.</strong> Pressure is what breaks the bond between dirt and the surface. It is what lifts embedded grime out of concrete pores and strips flaking paint. Too much of it is also what carves visible stripes into concrete and cuts through wood fibers on a deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>GPM is gallons per minute, the volume of water flowing.</strong> Volume is what carries loosened dirt away and rinses the surface. It is also what determines how large an area you can cover per pass. A high-pressure, low-volume machine breaks dirt loose in a narrow line and then has to be walked back and forth endlessly to actually clear it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cleaning units combine them, and it is the number that predicts real-world speed.</strong> Multiply PSI by GPM to get cleaning units. A 2000 PSI machine at 1.4 GPM produces 2,800 cleaning units. A 3100 PSI machine at 2.4 GPM produces 7,400. That machine is not 55 percent faster, it is more than twice as effective, and on a large driveway that difference is measured in hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>What to look for by task.</strong> Vehicles and delicate surfaces need roughly 1,500 to 2,000 PSI and low cleaning units. Siding, fences, and decks are comfortable in the 2,000 to 2,500 PSI range. Concrete driveways and masonry want 3,000 PSI and above with at least 2.0 GPM, and below roughly 5,000 cleaning units a driveway becomes a genuinely slow job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Higher is not automatically better.</strong> A 4000 PSI machine used carelessly will permanently damage wood, siding, window seals, and paint, and can cause serious injection injuries to skin. Buying more pressure than your surfaces can tolerate means buying a tool you must deliberately restrain, which is a real risk in practice.
          </p>

