        <div id="gas-vs-electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Gas vs Electric Pressure Washers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The choice determines both what you can clean and how much the machine will demand of you over its life. Unlike many outdoor tools, the gap here has not fully closed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Pressure Washers</h3>

          <p>
            Gas machines dominate on water volume, which is the specification electric units cannot match. A typical gas washer moves 2.4 to 3.5 GPM against 1.2 to 2.0 for electric, and because volume carries dirt away, that gap translates directly into cleaning speed on large surfaces. Gas is also completely portable, with no cord limiting where you work. The costs are noise, engine maintenance, fuel storage and stabilization, weight, and size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Pressure Washers</h3>

          <p>
            Electric machines start with a switch, run far quieter, need no fuel or oil, and store in a fraction of the space. Good ones with induction motors and 2000 PSI and up handle siding, decks, fences, vehicles, and patio furniture completely competently. The limitations are water volume, a power cord that caps working range, and the fact that cheap units with universal motors have genuinely short lives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Decide</h3>

          <p>
            If your main job is a concrete driveway or a large patio, buy gas or the most capable electric unit you can, and buy a surface cleaner regardless. If you mainly wash siding, cars, decks, and furniture, a quality electric machine is quieter, easier to store, and entirely sufficient. If you want one machine for everything and have somewhere to keep it, gas is the more versatile choice.
          </p>

