        <!-- SECTION 4: Nozzles, Surface Cleaners, and Attachments -->
        <div id="nozzles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Nozzles, Surface Cleaners, and Attachments
          </h2>

          <p>
            The nozzle on the end of the wand changes the effective pressure at the surface more than any setting on the machine, and choosing the wrong one is the leading cause of pressure washer damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Standard Nozzle Colors</h3>

          <p>
            Red is 0 degrees, a pencil-thin jet that concentrates all the pressure into a tiny point. It will carve concrete and should almost never be used. Yellow is 15 degrees for heavy stripping on hard surfaces. Green is 25 degrees, the general-purpose choice for concrete and driveways. White is 40 degrees for siding, vehicles, and delicate surfaces. Black is a low-pressure soap nozzle that draws detergent through the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Turbo or Rotary Nozzle</h3>

          <p>
            A turbo nozzle spins a 0-degree jet in a rapid cone, delivering the striking power of a zero-degree tip across the coverage of a 25-degree fan. It is genuinely the fastest way to clean concrete with a standard wand and is included with some machines. It is also aggressive enough to damage wood and siding, so keep it on hard surfaces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surface Cleaners Are the Real Upgrade</h3>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner is a shrouded disc with two or more rotating jets underneath that you push across flat concrete like a floor buffer. It roughly doubles cleaning speed on driveways and patios, eliminates the striping that hand-waving a wand always produces, and dramatically reduces overspray. If you clean any significant area of flat concrete, this is the single most worthwhile accessory available.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Wands and Gutter Attachments</h3>

          <p>
            Telescoping wands let you reach second-story siding and soffits from the ground, which is safer than working from a ladder with a hose that produces recoil. Curved gutter attachments clean gutters from the ground too. Both are inexpensive and remove genuinely dangerous ladder work.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Why Your Driveway Has Stripes</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Cleaning flat concrete by waving a wand back and forth always produces visible stripes, because the pressure at the center of the fan is higher than at the edges and overlap is never perfect. The stripes are not dirt, they are differential cleaning. A surface cleaner solves it completely by applying uniform pressure across its full width, which is why professionals use one on every flat surface.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: What You Can and Cannot Pressure Wash -->
        <div id="what-to-wash" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What You Can and Cannot Pressure Wash
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure washers cause a large amount of accidental property damage every year, almost all of it avoidable by knowing which surfaces tolerate what.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete and masonry.</strong> The best candidates. Use 3,000 PSI and a 25-degree nozzle or a surface cleaner. Avoid a zero-degree tip, which will etch permanent lines into the surface that no amount of subsequent cleaning removes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vinyl and fiber cement siding.</strong> Use a 40-degree nozzle at moderate pressure and spray at a slight downward angle, never upward, because water forced upward gets behind the siding and into the wall cavity where it causes rot and mold. Keep the wand at least a foot away.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wood decks and fences.</strong> The most commonly damaged surface. Wood fibers are soft and high pressure carves visible furrows and raises the grain permanently. Use a 40-degree nozzle at 1,500 PSI or less, keep the wand moving, and work with the grain. A deck cleaner applied first does most of the work chemically so you need far less pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vehicles.</strong> Use no more than about 1,500 PSI with a 40-degree nozzle, keep at least two feet away, and never aim directly at seals, badges, or trim. A foam cannon and a proper wash mitt do a better job on paint than pressure alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never pressure wash:</strong> asphalt shingles, which lose their protective granules and fail early; old mortar, which blows out of joints; painted surfaces you intend to keep, since pressure strips paint readily; windows and their seals; electrical panels, meters, and outdoor outlets; and anything with a person, pet, or plant in the path of the stream.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Personal safety matters more than people assume.</strong> A high-pressure stream can inject water and contaminants under the skin, producing an injury that looks minor and requires emergency surgery. Never point a wand at anyone, wear closed shoes and eye protection, and be aware that recoil on a high-output machine is strong enough to unbalance you on a ladder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Pump Care, Winterizing, and Common Failures -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pump Care, Winterizing, and Common Failures
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure washers almost always fail at the pump, and almost all of those failures come from three specific mistakes that are easy to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not run the machine in bypass for long.</strong> When the engine or motor is running and you are not squeezing the trigger, water circulates inside the pump and heats up rapidly. Sustained bypass is the single most common cause of pump failure. Machines with a total stop system shut off automatically; on machines without one, shut it down if you will be more than a minute or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never starve the pump for water.</strong> A pressure washer needs a strong, uninterrupted water supply. An undersized or kinked hose, a partially open spigot, or a long narrow hose run starves the pump, causing cavitation that damages seals and valves quickly. Use a short 5/8-inch or 3/4-inch hose from a fully open spigot, and turn the water on before starting the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Winterize with pump antifreeze.</strong> Water left in a pump freezes and cracks the housing, which is a terminal failure on a sealed consumer pump. After the last use of the season, run the machine briefly with pump saver antifreeze drawn through it, which both displaces water and lubricates the seals. This is a five-minute job that saves the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check and clean the inlet filter.</strong> A small screen at the water inlet catches grit that would otherwise reach the pump. It clogs gradually, restricting flow and starving the pump without any obvious symptom other than reduced pressure. Pull it and rinse it a few times a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store the hose properly.</strong> High-pressure hose is stiff and develops permanent kinks if coiled tightly or crushed under something heavy. A kinked high-pressure hose can burst under load, which is both dangerous and expensive. Coil it in large loops and hang it rather than leaving it in a pile.
          </p>
        </div>

