              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is PSI or GPM more important?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both matter, and the useful figure is cleaning units, which is PSI multiplied by GPM. Pressure breaks dirt loose while water volume carries it away and covers area. A 3100 PSI machine at 2.4 GPM produces 7,400 cleaning units and cleans more than twice as fast as a 2000 PSI machine at 1.4 GPM, despite the pressure difference looking modest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI do I need for a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for 3,000 PSI or more with at least 2.0 GPM, which puts you above roughly 6,000 cleaning units. Below about 5,000 cleaning units a concrete driveway becomes a genuinely slow job. Pair whatever machine you buy with a surface cleaner attachment, which roughly doubles speed on flat concrete and eliminates striping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I pressure wash a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, carefully. Use a 40-degree nozzle at 1,500 PSI or less, keep the wand moving, work with the grain, and stay at least a foot away. Wood fibers are soft and higher pressure carves permanent furrows and raises the grain. Applying a deck cleaner first does most of the work chemically so far less pressure is needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric pressure washers powerful enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For siding, decks, fences, vehicles, and patio furniture, absolutely. A quality electric unit with an induction motor at 2000 PSI and above handles all of that competently. For large concrete areas they are usable but slower, because their water volume of 1.2 to 2.0 GPM is well below the 2.4 to 3.5 GPM a gas machine delivers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my pump fail?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most commonly from running in bypass, meaning the machine running while the trigger is released, which heats the circulating water and destroys seals. The other frequent causes are starving the pump with an inadequate water supply, which causes cavitation, and freezing over winter without running pump antifreeze through it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
