        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Pressure Washer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure washers cause more accidental property damage than any other homeowner tool, and the errors are remarkably consistent.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Zero-Degree Nozzle on Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The red zero-degree tip concentrates all the machine's pressure into a pencil-thin point, and dragging it across concrete etches permanent lines that no future cleaning removes. It exists for very specific spot work and should essentially never be used on a driveway. Use a 25-degree tip or, far better, a surface cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Siding Upward</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Siding is designed to shed water running down it, and forcing water upward drives it behind the panels into the wall cavity where it causes rot and mold that appears months later. Always spray at a slight downward angle, use a 40-degree nozzle, and keep the wand at least a foot from the surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Machine Running Between Passes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running with the trigger released circulates water inside the pump, and that water heats rapidly. Sustained bypass operation is the leading cause of pump failure, and it happens constantly when people leave the machine idling while they move a ladder or coil a hose. Shut it down for anything longer than a minute.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Winterization</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water left in a pump freezes, expands, and cracks the housing, which ends the machine. Running pump saver antifreeze through the unit after the last use of the season takes five minutes, costs very little, and also lubricates the seals. This is the single highest-value maintenance task on a pressure washer.
              </p>
            </div>
